WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.316
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.650
- Hello, I'm Laura Sanders,

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.690
Senior Neuroscience
Writer at Science News.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.270
And it's a big year here
for us at Science News.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.210
It's our 100th anniversary

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.030
as an independent news
organization covering science.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:28.030
And so to mark the occasion
we're talking with leaders

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:31.800
in the sciences on the big
questions in their field.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.940
So I'm here with Sujata Gupta
our Social Science Writer

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.740
to talk about stress and resilience,

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.610
and how it affects our brains, our bodies,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.930
and even our societies.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.260
We're joined today by Dr. Huda Akil,

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.990
Professor of Neuroscience and Psychiatry,

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:52.890
and a member of the Michigan
Neuroscience Institute

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.360
at the University of
Michigan in Ann Arbor.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.940
She studies both stress and
resilience in the brain,

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.190
and over her wide-ranging career,

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.770
she's discovered how stress in
our world affects our brains,

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:07.830
and she's also turned
up ways to counter it.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.450
Dr. Akil thank you very much

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.580
for joining us.
- Thank you.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.550
- And I'll now turn

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.620
to my fellow science news
writer, Sujata Gupta.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.210
- Hi, as Laura said,

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.800
I'm Sujata Gupta a Social Sciences Writer,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.930
and my guest today is Dr. Brendan Kohrt.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.340
He's a Psychiatrist and Anthropologist

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.530
at George Washington University

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.310
and a global mental health expert

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.990
who has designed mental
health interventions

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.770
to help child soldiers
re-integrate into society in Nepal,

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.580
and programs to reduce the stigma

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.750
associated with mental illness
elsewhere in the world.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.900
Dr. Kohrt thank you so
much for joining us.

00:01:44.148 --> 00:01:47.100
- So, I'll turn to Dr. Akil first.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.760
And I think, you know, this year,

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.700
this past two years, especially,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.480
we've all heard people
say I'm so stressed out

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.700
and we all know what
that means intuitively,

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.700
but from your perspective, you know,

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.943
what does that mean from a
neuroscience point of view?

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:05.442
- Thank you, yeah, it's great to be here.

00:02:05.442 --> 00:02:08.170
The stress system, stress is of course

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:11.490
a psychological concept
that we all experience

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.840
and it indicates a sense
of discomfort, alarm,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.820
sometimes fear, anxiety,
uncertainty, and it carries

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.260
a lot of negative connotations.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.980
It is also a piece of
biology that we share

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.030
with pretty much all mammals.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.950
It's very clearly wired,
we have in our body

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.670
the ability to perceive the world,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.180
and encode whether something

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.620
is going to be stressful or not.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.520
And then there are
biochemical consequences

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.100
of this, where the encoding of an event

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.210
in the world around us, or even within us

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:56.210
says, pay attention, this is
threatening, this is important.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.390
And then that information in the brain

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.970
goes into a final end point
called the hypothalamus,

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.230
which releases a special hormone

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.920
that then turns on a cascade
of other hormones in our body,

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.430
circulates in the blood,
turns on a stress hormone

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:17.960
in the end called cortisol in the humans.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.830
And cortisol is a chemical

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.870
then that goes around all over the body,

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:27.010
goes back to our brain
and says, do something.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.600
And sometimes the
information is to alert you

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.470
to pay attention, to act,
to run away or to focus

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:38.630
or to fight, that's the
fight and flight response,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:43.320
and then eventually is to
say, stop worrying it's over.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.420
So, it's a very dynamic response

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.570
that encodes the information
in a biological way

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.810
and both encodes the
beginning and the end of it.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:57.920
And it's a system that you
find in a dog, in a cat,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.840
in a mouse, in a rat, in
a human, you know, monkey.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.753
It's very nicely conserved.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:06.613
- Thank you, Dr. Akil.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:10.010
So we're going to zoom out
a little from the brain

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:11.933
to all of society.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.330
And I'd just love to understand Dr. Kohrt

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:19.640
how you define stress from a
behavior science perspective.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.270
So for instance I've seen that, you know,

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.770
there's lots of articles lately
about being more mindful,

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.170
practicing yoga, you know, sort of staying

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.460
in the moment as an individual,

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.060
but your work really
suggests the importance

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.370
of community to building resilience,

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.140
and I was hoping you could explain that.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.030
- Yeah, I would be delighted

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.180
to, and I thank you so
much Sujata and Laura

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.450
for the opportunity to be here

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.393
and happy 100th anniversary
to Science News.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.010
So, concepts of stress and resilience

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.370
are often thought of in
terms of the individual,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.460
that something is happening
to me I am stressed out.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.120
And I think throughout the pandemic

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.300
we've all heard these
messages of self-care,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.900
take care of yourself, do some meditation,

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.080
do some exercise, try to eat right.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.670
So the idea of it's kind of,
this is an internal thing

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:10.730
happening to me, and then I'm
supposed to respond to it.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:13.640
I think of my, the own--
the hospital where I work

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.170
and they gave us all free
access to mindfulness apps

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.310
throughout the pandemic which is great.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:21.560
But as an anthropologist,
I also think about stress

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.900
and resilience as part of systems.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.150
They're part of families,
part of communities,

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.430
part of societies, part of nations.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:31.690
If we think ourselves in kind
of an interconnected web,

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.820
then that stress that's on the system

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.960
is also going to affect me.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.500
But in terms of resilience,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.080
it's really freeing to think
about that because the idea

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.090
is the system is resilient,
not just the individual.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:47.010
I've done a lot of work as
a humanitarian practitioner

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.460
in disaster affected settings,

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:50.810
after an earthquake, after a civil war.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.060
And what I've seen is
it's really the community

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.410
that determines how
people respond to stress

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.280
rather than any one individual.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.320
I really loved the comment
that Huda made about you know,

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.973
our brains telling us the trauma is over,

00:06:04.003 --> 00:06:05.870
the stress is over to stop freaking out.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:07.210
And that's something that occurs,

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.670
not only internally with regard

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.880
to our endocrine regulation in our brains,

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.310
but we rely on our friends on
our family, on our coworkers

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.550
to say the same thing to us.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:18.900
Hey, they know you're through this,

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.700
you've made it through that, you're able

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.150
to take care of yourself.

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:24.200
So resilience is constantly something

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.400
that we're doing inside and
with everybody else around us

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.640
at the same time.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.100
Which is why I think it's so important

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.680
to talk about it right now,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.410
because we're in the midst of
a pandemic where in many ways

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.530
we've been cut off from those
social connections around us

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.193
that help us respond to
stress and be resilient.

00:06:41.846 --> 00:06:44.210
- Fascinating.
- Yeah, that idea,

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:46.970
of kind of looking at
this at different levels,

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:50.150
you know, you can go all
the way into the cortisol

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.700
released in a certain brain area,

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:54.350
you could zoom out to
the person and the body,

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.840
and then you can zoom out
to the societal effects

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:59.563
and how we're all interconnected, it's,

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:04.460
I'm curious how you two see
those levels interacting,

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:09.460
you know, is there discoveries
to be had at all the levels?

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:10.810
Are they interconnected?

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:12.730
How do you see them kind
of working together?

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:13.563
- So, if I may,

00:07:15.438 --> 00:07:19.910
the question is what is stressful?

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.060
Let's think about humans
in particular for a minute.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:26.100
And I remember when I first started,

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.250
I would try to do things
to human subjects,

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.190
including putting them in a cold room

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:34.370
or asking them math questions.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:39.007
And one day my assistant said, you know,

00:07:39.007 --> 00:07:42.600
"The people who give us a
biological stress response

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.560
are doing it, especially if
they think we're judging them

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.490
about how they're doing
on their math questions,

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.920
not so much how hard the math problem is."

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.550
And sort of the penny drop that they,

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:57.600
that a lot of it is social.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.810
That being judged for example,

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:04.070
is really biologically what's
triggering the stress system.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.537
And then we started doing
studies in animals as well.

00:08:06.537 --> 00:08:10.190
And we realized how
much social interaction

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:14.290
is a key component of the stress system.

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:19.180
So if you think about the
job of surviving altogether,

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.530
exactly what Brendan said
is absolutely critical.

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.410
You're not alone in the world,

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:28.510
whether you're in the wild
or in a modern society,

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:31.310
and you need to take
care of whether yourself

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:35.060
or your babies or your property,

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:38.610
you need to take the
environment into consideration

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.400
and that includes the social environment.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.693
So, social encoding is absolutely key

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:50.930
to, we have entire brain
systems, brain circuits

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:55.510
that encode relationships,
support, hierarchy,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.710
aggression, social interactions.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:02.060
So these ideas are completely compatible

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:06.280
because the social setting and the biology

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:11.280
are all part of that same
survival evolutionary framework

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.363
that we're all living in.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.630
- I mean, it seems really intuitive.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:22.930
But, I think it's easy to
forget that we're social beings.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:26.600
And I'm curious Dr. Kohrt,
when we spoke earlier

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.710
you mentioned that, you know,
the sort of therapist client,

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.370
your psychiatrist and the
therapist client relationship

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:33.463
is very individual.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:36.120
And I'm just wondering how you began

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.320
to look at this as more
of a social question,

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.883
stress and resilience as
more of a social question.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:46.960
- As a psychiatrist going
through years of training

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:49.040
to become a good therapist,

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:50.920
we always think about the relationship

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:53.500
of that person who's
going through distress,

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:54.930
sitting in a room with me together

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:56.560
and how we talked about problems.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:59.240
And you get trained a lot on those skills,

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.620
and then being able to
teach an individual skills

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:04.520
to help respond to the
distress in their lives.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:06.210
And so for most of my clinical training,

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:09.410
that was how I thought about the idea

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.880
of kind of healing and recovery.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.140
But in terms of my anthropological work

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:15.800
in different places around the world,

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.860
I can remember at this point
almost 25 years ago exactly.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:22.490
It was my first trip to Southern Nepal

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.660
to see how people with mental illness

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:26.730
dealt with the suffering and recovered.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:30.140
And there I saw a small
temple built in the community.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.810
There were no psychiatrists,
no psychologists available,

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:34.240
and people who were in distress,

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:37.540
some that we might diagnose
with depression, some with PTSD,

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:39.210
even some with more severe conditions

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:41.420
like mania and bipolar disorder,

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.410
I saw them come to this
temple and spend time together

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:48.850
to go through rituals together
to heal and process this .

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.300
Family members would also come

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:52.180
from time to time to support them.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:55.380
And although by no means did
it kind of cure everything,

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:58.730
it was really incredible to
see in the absence of even,

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:02.210
you know, most medications
and the supports that way,

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.040
there were still ways to support people

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:05.760
and help them heal together.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.940
And I've seen since then
really great combinations

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.410
of when you have medicines available,

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:12.240
but you also have these
great community supports

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.710
that get mobilized.

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:18.020
Conversely, I've seen the reverse happen.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.830
In my experience working
with child soldiers,

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:23.360
what was surprising to me
is even though many of them

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:25.520
had gone through absolutely, you know,

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:29.510
awful traumatic events, not
all of them really did poorly.

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:31.140
Some of them came back
to their communities

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:32.920
and they did really quite well

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.060
after such awful things happening to them,

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:38.220
but other child soldiers we
saw in Nepal came back home,

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:39.140
and really suffered

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:42.500
from chronic debilitating
post-traumatic stress disorder

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:45.290
and other forms of psychological ailments.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.970
And the difference wasn't the trauma.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:51.760
It was how the families,
schools, teachers, communities,

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.710
religious leaders responded
to those child soldiers

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:56.350
as they came back home.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.520
What we saw in the one
communities where they did well

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:00.650
was lots of support,
welcoming them back to school,

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:02.420
help them restart their lives,

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.860
and in other community
where there was not support

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:09.540
for them coming back,
former child soldiers

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.500
who were girls were
forced to sit on the floor

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.780
in the classroom, some
weren't even allowed

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:15.280
back in the schools.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.310
And what we saw was that it was actually

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:20.450
the social ostracization being excluded

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:23.320
from returning to society that
really made the difference

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:26.640
in terms of trauma leading
to psychological distress.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:29.220
And it really interesting
kind of a historical fact

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:32.777
is that, although we all talk
about PTSD all the time now,

00:12:32.777 --> 00:12:36.210
the PTSD as a diagnosis
really didn't exist

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:37.810
before the Vietnam war.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.310
It wasn't until around 1980,

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:42.410
when psychiatrists in
their typical manuals

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:46.310
included PTSD in large
part based on research done

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:48.140
with people returning from Vietnam,

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:50.500
where you had the same process in the US,

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:52.080
some people came back to communities,

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:54.630
got a lot of support from
their families and communities,

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:56.150
but in other places around the US

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:58.060
when people came back home as soldiers,

00:12:58.060 --> 00:12:59.610
they were shunned by communities,

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.400
they were not allowed to return,

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.630
they were mocked politically.

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:05.190
And really it's that experience,

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.360
that social response after trauma,

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:10.480
that really makes an
important determination

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.870
of whether trauma leads to distress,

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:14.390
or trauma can be something

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:16.490
that we recover from and are resilient to.

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:21.990
- It's a really
interesting idea, you know,

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:25.610
you bring up not just the
original traumatic experience,

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:27.300
but the response afterward,

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.370
and kind of this really
crucial time element.

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:32.940
And I wonder Dr. Akil,

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.830
you've written this
really nice perspective

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:39.530
on the kind of the history of
discovering the stress system

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:43.980
and kind of how these concepts
have changed over time.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:45.500
So I'm wondering if you could tell us

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.850
a little bit about that progression

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:49.260
of our understanding of it.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:51.980
You know, it's not just a simple fight

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:55.950
or flight discreet event,
but it seems rather

00:13:55.950 --> 00:14:00.950
to be this more kind of
interactive, adaptive concept.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:04.720
- So, if you step back and think about

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.610
what the job is of the brain in general,

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:10.710
you know, especially as you get

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:15.113
to more complicated organisms
like mammals, humans.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:21.640
The job is to recognize the environment,

00:14:22.048 --> 00:14:22.950
cope with it,

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:26.030
get the best out of it,
avoid the worst out of it.

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:29.088
So it is really about

00:14:29.088 --> 00:14:32.021
perceiving, remembering, assessing,

00:14:32.021 --> 00:14:36.110
responding, learning
so that you can evolve

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:39.320
and even predicting what's
going to happen in the future

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:41.693
so you avoid the pain and agony.

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:47.360
That job sits really at the
interface between the individual

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:48.930
and the world around it.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:52.000
And that's the physical world,
that's the social world.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:55.190
The stress system is exactly that system.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.350
It's not genes alone, it's
not environment alone,

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:02.560
it's a system that
assesses the environment.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:07.320
And through evolution, we
have multiple mechanisms

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:09.140
of how to cope.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:12.290
Some of them are actually defensive.

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:14.730
It's, you know, where
you just go in a corner

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.230
and hide from threat, for example.

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:20.710
And some are more, you know, exploratory,

00:15:20.710 --> 00:15:24.580
like you are willing to take a
risk because it might be fun,

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:28.290
or it might be something
productive out there.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:32.480
And in a population, there
is a distribution of people.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:34.840
Some tend to be more conservative

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.567
in the way they interact with the world,

00:15:36.567 --> 00:15:41.430
and some might be more
extroverted, interactive,

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:42.900
risk-taking and so on.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.800
And there is no right and wrong.

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:47.240
It's good for the population at large,

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:49.780
to have different kinds of reactions

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:53.500
to the world of interacting
with each other.

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:57.460
But if the point of this whole thing

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:00.320
is to be constantly
assessing the environment,

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:03.610
then we can't think of it
only as an alarm system.

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:05.210
It's not just your alarm

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:08.780
that's sitting there doing
nothing until a horrible,

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:12.500
like the fire, you know,
there's a fire, or, you know,

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:17.500
an attack, it has to be monitoring
and adapting constantly.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:20.500
And so, I think, and we see

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:22.910
that even the level of monitoring changes

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:25.130
during the course of the day for example,

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:26.650
and in different settings.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.900
So it's a very much alive, ongoing system

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.790
that does good things
as well as bad things.

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:38.790
And the good things are this
kind of very adaptive plastic

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.450
malleable way of learning.

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:45.190
And the more complicated the creature,

00:16:45.190 --> 00:16:48.270
the more actually noise
there is in the system,

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:50.737
because the more
unpredictable the environment

00:16:50.737 --> 00:16:53.900
and the richer the opportunities are.

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.290
Whereas if you are ultra programmed

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:59.930
like a monarch butterfly,
it's beautifully programmed

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.870
way better than we are,
but then the environment

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:04.340
can vary by much.

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:08.530
So you build all this kind of elasticity,

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:11.680
plasticity, adaptability into the system.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:14.870
But the price for that
is it's never perfect.

00:17:14.870 --> 00:17:19.178
It's always kind of trying to catch up

00:17:19.178 --> 00:17:20.930
with what is going on.

00:17:20.930 --> 00:17:24.090
It can do that for short
periods really well,

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:26.420
and that's what we call good stress.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:30.000
Where something happens,
you learn from it,

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.910
you get a swift stress response,

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:36.040
it makes you pay attention,
it makes you remember,

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:39.880
it makes you change your
behavior, and respond accordingly.

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:43.060
But if it keeps going and going and going,

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:46.100
and there is no relief
either within yourself

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:48.670
or through your support system,

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:51.950
then it's cumulative and
the cost is biological

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:55.200
and very expensive and literally changes

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.330
the fabric of the brain.

00:17:57.330 --> 00:18:01.830
And so, again, very highly intertwined

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:03.940
with what the environment is doing,

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:05.820
and time is really important.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:07.860
So your point about time,

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:10.320
the points that have been made about time.

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:13.540
The sequence of events,
the duration of the event,

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:16.890
the direction that they're
going, are they cumulative?

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:18.910
Are they letting off at times?

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:19.930
Are you having time

00:18:19.930 --> 00:18:22.720
to integrate the
information and restart it?

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.980
All of that can be seen
both psychologically,

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:29.433
and we know some biological
correlates for it.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.123
- Yeah, as you were saying,

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:38.170
stress that goes on and on, I
had thoughts of the pandemic,

00:18:38.530 --> 00:18:41.133
and we've all experienced
this for a long, long time.

00:18:41.133 --> 00:18:43.720
- That's right.
- And I know both of you

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:45.840
have worked with animals.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.310
And I'd be curious if you could, you know,

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:52.170
compare what you know from
laboratory studies, you know,

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:54.710
what things do you do to the animals

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:58.220
to elicit a stress response,
and how might that compare

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.590
with what we're all experiencing now?

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:04.620
You know, you've already
touched on the isolation,

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:09.240
the long duration, the uncertainty
about future, you know,

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:11.833
are there parallels you can tell us about?

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.230
- Brendan you want to
start and then I'll follow?

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:20.980
- Sure, so my direct animal
research days are long behind me

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:25.700
I deal with the bipedal
human animals these days.

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:29.390
But the animal research
has really been incredible

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:31.880
to inform how we think about this.

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:32.860
And I really take kind

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:35.850
of a biocultural evolutionary perspective.

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.560
So the idea of what we're
trying to understand

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:40.780
about stress resilience and healing,

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:42.710
we can look across evolution

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:44.640
not just our biological evolution,

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.810
but also how cultures have evolved.

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:49.500
And if we think about
evolutionary processes,

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:51.790
evolutions doesn't just act on individuals

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:52.960
it acts on groups.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:54.050
So we can actually see

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.730
that in, especially in social mammals,

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:59.070
the evolution of these social behaviors

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:00.763
to respond to stress.

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:04.540
If we think about kind of healing,

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:07.790
one of the best images that
comes to mind are, you know,

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:10.720
the images that Jane Goodall described

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:13.330
working with chimpanzees
of grooming behaviors.

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:16.280
That if you look at somebody
distressed in a group,

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:18.160
a distressed, you know, adolescent

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:21.650
or a distressed female
partner or male partner,

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:23.750
that often it's through grooming,

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:26.190
that people would calm down,
that it would, that would,

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:27.690
or the chimpanzees would calm down.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:30.768
That was the behavior that
formed an alliance again,

00:20:30.768 --> 00:20:33.550
they got a chimpanzee who is distressed

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:35.840
to calm and be able to interact.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:37.940
And if we look at this, it really

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:39.550
is a behavior that can be seen

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:43.460
across almost all social
mammals in some way.

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:46.490
A really interesting thing
is that in the 1990s,

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.920
there was a group of Dutch researchers,

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:50.600
and they were working
on creating, you know,

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.220
rat models of PTSD.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:56.080
And so they would put a rat in a cage

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:59.610
with a quite aggressive larger male rat,

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:02.860
and then have the rat go
back to its original cage,

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:03.780
and then look and see

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:07.703
if it had a lot of the behaviors of PTSD.

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:10.160
And what they found

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:14.210
was that when they were trying
to replicate this behavior

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:18.240
amongst rats, that they
weren't seeing it initially,

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:20.960
and they were saying, "Well,
why is that not the case?"

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:24.360
And what they noticed is typically
they were taking the rats

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:25.840
and then putting them back

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:28.690
with other rats with
whom they were familiar.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:29.950
If they took the rat,

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:32.600
had that rat exposed to
that aggressive male rat,

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:36.210
and then put that rat back
alone, suddenly all those,

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:37.110
a lot of the behaviors

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.240
that we think of when we think of PTSD,

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:43.150
avoidance, hyper arousal to any stimuli,

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:47.460
and even the hypothalamic
pituitary adrenal stress response

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:49.100
that Huda was just describing,

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:52.050
that you can recreate all
of that in a rat model.

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:55.940
But it was particularly in
the rats who were caged alone

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:59.200
after a stressor that you
really saw this response.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:02.430
The other thing is that we can
also think about the biology

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:06.300
of healing and how that's
kind of evolutionary

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.250
in our past and ourselves now.

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:10.560
That there's a whole process that happens

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:12.240
when we as a human being,

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:14.500
or again amongst amongst chimps or rats

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:15.890
or other social mammals,

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:18.180
when we see another member of our species

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:20.930
or our in group in
particular that's distressed,

00:22:20.930 --> 00:22:22.960
we also feel that stress.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:26.730
We have our own stress
response to others in distress.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:30.200
When that happens, we use
our own internal mechanisms

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:32.070
to begin to calm ourselves down,

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:35.130
but because we're cued
to somebody else's stress

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:37.010
through mirror neurons
and other processes,

00:22:37.010 --> 00:22:39.020
we then engage with them.

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:41.070
We reach out, we try to help.

00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:43.460
And that actually by giving support,

00:22:43.460 --> 00:22:45.730
not only does their stress reduce,

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:47.180
but there's actually a feedback loop

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:48.930
where we get the kind of that warm glow

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:52.410
that nice fuzzy feeling
from helping somebody else.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:55.490
And that causes us to repeatedly engage

00:22:55.490 --> 00:22:57.640
in that support of other people.

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:00.820
And that's really the flow
of empathy in our species

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:01.930
that helps us to realize

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.600
that we're not just
alone healing ourselves,

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:08.100
but we're responsible to be
healed, to heal other people,

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:10.210
but also should be
receptive to being healed

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:14.463
by others in our families,
communities, and social lives.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:19.440
- Yeah, so, I want to
echo some of the things

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:20.350
that Brendan said.

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:23.560
One is that we have an
unpublished studies in animal

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:26.740
that shows that effect of community.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:31.564
Not only on the kind of

00:23:31.564 --> 00:23:33.960
isolation after the fact,

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:37.150
but even during a stressful event.

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:42.150
If we take animals that are
genetically have a tendency

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:46.340
to be very inhibited and very, you know,

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:48.250
passive and inhibited,

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:51.270
and we put them through an experience

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:54.710
that is negative for
them, it really matters

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:56.610
who else is in the room with them,

00:23:56.610 --> 00:24:00.160
even if they're in separate
cages and pretty separated.

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:02.500
The same experience if they undergo it

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.210
during the experience itself alone,

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:08.980
versus in a group produces
very different brain patterns

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:11.370
and very different consequences.

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:13.640
We don't know how they're communicating,

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:15.910
through smell for example,

00:24:15.910 --> 00:24:18.360
or through ultrasonic vocalization.

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:20.990
But we again discovered this by chance

00:24:20.990 --> 00:24:23.500
because we had different studies

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:25.260
and they were giving us different results

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:27.720
and we tracked it down to the fact

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:30.620
that whether you're
experiencing the stress alone

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:32.850
or in a group makes a big difference.

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:36.000
It's not only whether also
you're supported afterwards,

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:37.540
but even during.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:39.430
And it brings to mind what happens

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:41.690
if a whole group of people experiences

00:24:41.690 --> 00:24:44.050
a traumatic event together,

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:47.170
versus if you're alone
when you experience it.

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:49.580
So even animals show us that.

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:52.670
I think it was very cool to discover

00:24:52.670 --> 00:24:56.250
and we tracked down some
of the brain circuits

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:57.533
that get activated.

00:24:58.390 --> 00:25:02.820
The other thing I wanted to touch on

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.160
is this idea of healing,

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:09.820
but also of stress and
resilience in general,

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:11.660
we've been saying those terms,

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:15.820
and who experiences stress and how.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:20.360
And a lot of people might
think that being resilient

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:25.360
is not feeling stressed or
not feeling stressed as much.

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:29.433
And I think that's one kind of resilience.

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:33.880
And, but the other kind of resilience

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:37.050
is having counterregulatory mechanisms

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:41.820
to compensate for that stress
and rebalance yourself,

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:44.390
which I think I hear
Brendan kind of referring

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:47.330
to in a lot of these
cases, like, you know,

00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:50.280
being in a group, feeling supported.

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:55.180
And we've been very interested
in understanding mechanisms

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:58.230
of stress versus resilience,
different mechanisms,

00:25:58.230 --> 00:26:01.810
biological and psychological, behavioral,

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.670
both in animal models
and in human studies,

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:08.090
and in this case, these
are college freshmen.

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:10.860
And in both cases, it's very clear

00:26:12.747 --> 00:26:16.410
that there are genetic
biological mechanisms

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:20.500
of resilience that we see
both in animals and in humans.

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:24.830
But that there are environmental
mechanisms of resilience

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:27.393
that are pretty clearly independent,

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:31.460
and that will more than compensate

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.810
for the genetic vulnerability.

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:37.420
So we have animals that
are very vulnerable

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:39.420
to negative emotions,

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:42.580
then we also have college
freshmen who genetically,

00:26:42.580 --> 00:26:44.780
when you're looking at their genetic load,

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.073
look like they're going
to be very vulnerable.

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:50.690
And we've been studying
them through the pandemic,

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:52.320
before and through the pandemic

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:56.220
and looking at that interplay
between genes and environment

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:59.480
and depression, anxiety versus resilience.

00:26:59.480 --> 00:27:02.130
And it's very evident

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:04.890
that you can be very
genetically vulnerable

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:09.410
and yet socially and
psychologically resilient.

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:11.320
And that there are people who seem

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:14.040
to be very biologically resilient,

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:19.040
and under normal circumstances
like before the pandemic,

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:22.370
they sail through a typical freshman year

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:24.620
without experiencing too much distress

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.710
or anxiety or depression,

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:30.300
but the pandemic actually
hit them really hard.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:33.300
And it's as if they had not developed

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:36.290
some of the psychosocial tools

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:40.980
that people who are more
vulnerable might have developed

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:42.940
in order to survive.

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:44.300
And so this idea,

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:46.820
and we see that as I
said in animal models.

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:49.810
So there is this idea that
there are multiple paths

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:51.830
to achieving resilience,

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:55.260
as well as multiple ways to be vulnerable.

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:59.760
And genetics is not destiny,

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:04.490
and context really can
more than compensate.

00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:08.710
And that even some
experience in being stressed

00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:11.160
is actually resilience inducing

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:13.650
in a way that you don't see

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.887
if somebody never experienced distress

00:28:15.887 --> 00:28:18.173
and then suddenly gets hit pretty hard.

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:20.670
- That's fascinating, I kind of remember

00:28:20.670 --> 00:28:23.200
reading these articles
when the pandemic first hit

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:26.370
from people who suffered
from sort of extreme anxiety

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:29.190
saying, "I'm actually doing okay,

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:30.847
I've prepared for the worst."

00:28:33.020 --> 00:28:36.650
So sort of related to that,
I feel like the pandemic

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:39.800
is almost short-circuiting
like these coping mechanisms

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:43.830
that we have, and that we are
fortunately able to develop.

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:45.410
And, you know, like you said, you know,

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:47.070
genetics isn't destiny,

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:49.550
but I'm thinking like social distancing,

00:28:49.550 --> 00:28:51.460
this, and a lot of the
social science literature,

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:53.980
it's like the together apart.

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:57.090
And I just I'm wondering
how we can sort of apply

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:59.730
these, you know, sort of
more theoretical findings

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:01.050
to what we're seeing now

00:29:01.050 --> 00:29:04.283
with sort of these ruptures
in society with the pandemic.

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:11.150
- Exactly Sujata so, and
Huda your points as well

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:13.440
in terms of how certain college students

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:15.920
responded to stressors before the pandemic

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:17.920
and how this has really changed

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:21.290
our ways of interacting
with others, you know,

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:23.260
and to the point of people with, you know,

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:25.690
anxiety and how might
they have responded before

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:27.087
in some ways it's almost,

00:29:27.087 --> 00:29:29.460
the pandemic was protective in some ways

00:29:29.460 --> 00:29:32.040
because they didn't have a
lot of those interactions.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:36.740
So I think in some ways
actually being forced

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:39.470
to interact with others
and spend time with them

00:29:39.470 --> 00:29:40.890
when you think about colleges, you know,

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:44.780
college students on a campus
or us in our daily lives,

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:46.990
dropping kids off at
school and picking them up,

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:49.200
they're all these little
micro interactions

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:52.160
that are just reassuring
back to Huda's point

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:55.230
about, you know, the experience
of a traumatic event.

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:57.690
Are you alone, or are you
going through it with others?

00:29:57.690 --> 00:30:00.630
And so I think the way
that society was built

00:30:00.630 --> 00:30:02.120
prior to the pandemic,

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:04.380
we went through a lot of things together.

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:05.510
And in many ways now

00:30:05.510 --> 00:30:08.080
we've been having to go
through things alone.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:10.390
So the question is, how do we find ways

00:30:10.390 --> 00:30:13.080
to be able to do that?

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:14.470
It doesn't necessarily force us

00:30:14.470 --> 00:30:16.010
into a distressful situation,

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:18.500
but helps us feel not so isolated.

00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:22.100
And in my work really it's about
kind of the flow of empathy

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.210
and how do we promote the flow of empathy

00:30:24.210 --> 00:30:26.720
during especially a humanitarian disaster

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:28.800
like a global pandemic.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:31.850
So two things have come
up in our work recently

00:30:31.850 --> 00:30:32.880
that have been really important.

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:37.370
So one, is the recognition
that particular individuals

00:30:37.370 --> 00:30:38.970
have really been isolated

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:41.950
from accessing mental health
care throughout the pandemic,

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:44.710
especially communities, BIPOC communities,

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:46.800
so black and brown
communities, indigenous,

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:48.370
and other peoples of color.

00:30:48.370 --> 00:30:51.410
That in many ways those individuals
had to access, you know,

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:54.080
things in person, there was a loss

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:56.820
of that, and then having to
shift over to remoteness.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:30:59.770
But then the other piece was
it has been a recognition

00:30:59.770 --> 00:31:02.920
in the past year that
we have a huge disparity

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:06.250
into who actually provides
mental health services.

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:08.990
That the US is 40% minorities,

00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:12.920
but only one out of every
10 mental health specialists

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:13.930
is not white.

00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:15.750
And so how can we have people

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:17.410
that have shared lived experience

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:20.110
come from shared
communities, shared cultures,

00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:21.970
when our mental health professionals

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:25.100
do not represent the makeup
of the American public.

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:27.540
So we've tried to find
ways to actually shift

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:29.330
how mental health care is delivered,

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:31.900
and shift who's delivering
mental health care.

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:34.040
So we've taken interventions
that we've used

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:36.010
after an earthquake in Nepal,

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:38.370
or after a war in other setting,

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:41.320
and adapted those actually
for use in New York City,

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:43.160
and training up people who work

00:31:43.160 --> 00:31:46.740
in community-based
organizations, religious centers,

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:49.080
other types of community-based bodies

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:53.130
to be able to provide brief
psychological support,

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:55.750
so you have someone from
the same community as you

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:57.110
able to support you.

00:31:57.110 --> 00:31:58.860
And we know that that really promotes

00:31:58.860 --> 00:32:01.200
that flow of empathy, understanding.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.670
Back to Huda's point, it's
like we are in this together

00:32:03.670 --> 00:32:05.730
going through these difficult situations.

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:09.090
It's not a substitute for when
a specialist might be needed

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:11.100
or something like
medication might be needed,

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:13.610
but it's really important
for that general level

00:32:13.610 --> 00:32:15.940
of psychological distress
that has just escalated

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:17.310
for all of us.

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:19.150
But another really important piece

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:21.610
about trying to make sure
that there's more care

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:24.050
and more empathy in the world,

00:32:24.050 --> 00:32:26.860
is also about our divisions in society

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:29.030
and stigma and discrimination.

00:32:29.030 --> 00:32:31.610
That if you look at people who are seen

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:33.500
as not part of your group,

00:32:33.500 --> 00:32:35.370
and when they go for it through suffering,

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:37.000
you don't have that same response

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:38.750
of kind of internalizing their suffering

00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:40.210
and reaching out to help them.

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:43.520
There's this, you know,
German term schadenfreude

00:32:43.520 --> 00:32:48.210
which is, you know,
actually even neurobiology,

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:50.050
neurobiologically rather true concept

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:52.640
where you can feel pleasure when somebody

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:55.590
who's not in your social
group is in pain, right?

00:32:55.590 --> 00:32:58.860
That's something about our
evolution made that adaptive.

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:00.450
Which means we need to find ways

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:02.570
to break down those group divisions.

00:33:02.570 --> 00:33:04.517
And so we've done a lot
of work on this in Nepal,

00:33:04.517 --> 00:33:07.690
we're now doing it in seven
countries around the world

00:33:07.690 --> 00:33:10.010
where in particular we're
trying to reduce the stigma

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:12.220
and discrimination
related to mental illness.

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:14.300
So we teach people living
with mental illness

00:33:14.300 --> 00:33:16.540
to tell their stories, to use photographs

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:18.660
or video or voice narratives,

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:20.100
to be able to share what it's like

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:21.540
and what they're going through.

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:22.860
And then we play these,

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:25.920
or have these individuals
come and give talks

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:27.010
to healthcare workers

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:29.160
who often are stigmatizing against them,

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:31.080
and they begin to find those connections,

00:33:31.080 --> 00:33:33.880
that idea that our lives
really aren't so far apart.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:36.100
You have the same concerns
about your kids, that I do,

00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:37.130
you have your same,

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:39.730
you enjoy the same festivals
and holidays that I do,

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:41.730
but trying to find those connections.

00:33:41.730 --> 00:33:43.870
And when you create those connections,

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:45.890
that empathy flow opens up.

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:47.540
So suddenly I feel your suffering

00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:50.380
and I want to respond in a
positive way to support you.

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:52.210
So I think the two really important things

00:33:52.210 --> 00:33:55.340
that we're recognizing as
a society now is that one,

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:56.950
we need to have a broader,

00:33:56.950 --> 00:33:59.440
much more diverse mental health workforce

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:01.360
to really represent the whole array

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:04.200
of different forms of
human, of lived experience

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:06.500
amongst us as a nation and as a society.

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:09.130
And the second is what tools can we use

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:11.760
to break down barriers of
stigma and discrimination

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:13.630
so that people are more empathetic,

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:15.620
caring and helpful towards one another,

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:18.120
especially when coming out of a, you know,

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:21.283
two year long potentially
longer pandemic at this point.

00:34:23.150 --> 00:34:25.730
- So, I want to also add something

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:30.000
that I've been thinking about, which is

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:33.650
I find very unusualabout this pandemic.

00:34:33.650 --> 00:34:37.900
We talk about isolation which
is of course the big stress

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.960
of loneliness, isolation, and so on.

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:43.000
But to me, an important question,

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:46.430
and I wonder how we will
be able to deal with it

00:34:46.430 --> 00:34:49.550
is how we perceive
ourselves and each other

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:50.770
in terms of rules.

00:34:50.770 --> 00:34:55.667
For example, I am a grandmother and I,

00:34:55.667 --> 00:34:58.443
before the pandemic took
some plan, and you know,

00:34:58.443 --> 00:35:00.200
I work full time, I'm very busy,

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:04.360
but I also took pleasure in
helping with my grandchildren

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:07.410
both to connect with them,

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:10.210
but also to take some
of the load of the back

00:35:10.210 --> 00:35:14.600
of my, you know, son and
daughter in law who live in town.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:17.330
During the pandemic, not allowed, right?

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:19.380
You can't play that role.

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:22.540
So, not only are parents of children

00:35:22.540 --> 00:35:26.070
overburdened because their
kids are at home, or, you know,

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:29.860
being sent home because
of somebody getting sick,

00:35:29.860 --> 00:35:32.270
but people in the community

00:35:32.270 --> 00:35:35.910
who used to be able to
help them, can't help them.

00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:38.200
So that cuts both ways actually, right?

00:35:38.200 --> 00:35:41.400
It cuts both because I feel helpless,

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:45.990
and they want to protect
me, but the whole nature

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:50.990
of who is capable of
giving empathy has changed.

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:55.180
And who you perceive as a
potential threat to you,

00:35:55.180 --> 00:35:58.200
like a friend who comes too close to you

00:35:58.200 --> 00:36:02.150
is, can be, if they're not
vaccinated, or if you have,

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:04.710
if you know somebody who
is immune compromised,

00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:09.710
all of these nuances of our
social fabric have changed.

00:36:10.060 --> 00:36:11.890
So it's not just isolation,

00:36:11.890 --> 00:36:16.300
it's how we're labeling each
other as sources of anxiety,

00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:18.910
as source of, people we need to protect

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:20.773
versus people who can be helpful.

00:36:21.620 --> 00:36:24.340
So, rules have changed.

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:27.330
And I think it's important to recognize

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:31.380
that and we empower some people,

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:34.540
and we support other
people who are feeling

00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:37.450
like their whole entire support system

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:41.160
had to be lost because they're
protecting somebody else.

00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:44.820
So, I don't know that we have
thought this through fully

00:36:44.820 --> 00:36:49.820
as a society, but it's actually quite,

00:36:50.648 --> 00:36:52.010
pervasive

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:55.383
and I think very problematic
at multiple levels.

00:36:57.500 --> 00:36:59.160
- Yeah, you raise a great point.

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:01.350
I know all of us have probably gone

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:03.350
through those kinds of re-evaluations

00:37:03.350 --> 00:37:05.370
of these are my children,

00:37:05.370 --> 00:37:08.110
but they're also unvaccinated threats

00:37:08.110 --> 00:37:09.570
to my parents right now.

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:12.680
You know, they're, how do I assess

00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:14.440
all of these various risks

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:18.137
and reevaluate how I see these people

00:37:18.137 --> 00:37:21.650
who have been such a profound
part of our fabric, you know.

00:37:21.650 --> 00:37:23.660
- And so.
- It changes it.

00:37:23.660 --> 00:37:25.920
- For me one reason I want to surface

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:28.700
this is it doesn't need
to get all the way.

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:31.290
So definitely the rates of depression

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:34.090
and anxiety and et cetera are going up

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:36.190
and we need to deal with those.

00:37:36.190 --> 00:37:39.970
But I also run a lab up
with a lot of young people,

00:37:39.970 --> 00:37:41.740
young women who have children,

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:45.730
and I see what, how demanding
that is, and I watch

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:48.333
my own children with
their children and so on.

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:52.720
And how do we socially support them

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:54.860
and also re-empower other people

00:37:54.860 --> 00:37:57.240
who used to play roles and no longer can.

00:37:57.240 --> 00:38:00.950
And I think it would be really good for us

00:38:00.950 --> 00:38:05.950
to start re-engaging in how we can

00:38:06.318 --> 00:38:09.330
create new roles or we
reformulate the roles

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:14.330
that we have in order to
evolve that social network

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:17.313
that has been completely upset.

00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:23.590
- One thing, we've
touched on a little bit,

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:26.500
but I would love more thoughts on,

00:38:26.500 --> 00:38:30.200
this idea of kind of cultivating control,

00:38:30.200 --> 00:38:34.330
cultivating something that
you derive pleasure from,

00:38:34.330 --> 00:38:36.440
that you feel competent at,

00:38:36.440 --> 00:38:38.263
that you're good at, that you enjoy.

00:38:39.620 --> 00:38:42.080
How does that fit in with
this idea of resiliency,

00:38:42.080 --> 00:38:44.650
and how important is that as a concept

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:48.540
to kind of the human psyche
or even our neurobiology?

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:50.150
You know, what does that do to our brain

00:38:50.150 --> 00:38:53.423
to have something like that,
that we feel good about?

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:59.160
- Yeah, I really liked
the point about Brendan,

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:03.780
the flow of empathy as a positive force.

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:07.420
I find that in neuroscience
and in psychiatry,

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:10.290
we think more about the bad things

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:12.500
and not enough about the good things.

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:13.727
That's not a scientific term, but like,

00:39:13.727 --> 00:39:16.340
you know, we think about
depression and anxiety

00:39:16.340 --> 00:39:18.830
and stress and addiction and so on,

00:39:18.830 --> 00:39:22.240
and we try to take people
from a negative state

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:25.450
to like a neutral state, and say,

00:39:25.450 --> 00:39:27.820
let's see if we can get rid of the anxiety

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:32.380
or decrease your depression
or minimize your stress,

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:34.460
but sometimes that's not possible.

00:39:34.460 --> 00:39:38.210
And so I have this concept that
I call it emotional algebra,

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:41.290
where you can, if you have negative,

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:44.850
you just need to counterbalance
it with something positive.

00:39:44.850 --> 00:39:47.830
And, you know, the negative could be huge

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:50.140
and the positives that have to be small,

00:39:50.140 --> 00:39:53.150
but many, maybe many of
them, many tiny positives,

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:56.710
sometimes a big positive,
but if you can't afford,

00:39:56.710 --> 00:40:01.360
so, but if you think that
you have a certain capital

00:40:01.360 --> 00:40:06.360
of emotionality, and it's
trending in a very negative way,

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:08.890
because of circumstances,

00:40:08.890 --> 00:40:13.890
why not try to put
something into your account?

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:16.790
That's more on the positive side.

00:40:16.790 --> 00:40:20.950
And that requires some paying
attention to the things,

00:40:20.950 --> 00:40:24.220
stopping long enough to pay attention

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:26.808
to the things that give you
a little bit of pleasure,

00:40:26.808 --> 00:40:27.808
a little bit of joy.

00:40:28.770 --> 00:40:30.280
And they can be about you,

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:32.090
they can be about people around you.

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:35.810
So for example I was
telling you, it's been hard

00:40:35.810 --> 00:40:37.540
not to be able to help family.

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:40.700
So, I try and give more donation

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:43.027
to people who are, you know, in need.

00:40:43.027 --> 00:40:46.720
You know, since, you
know, we were quarantined

00:40:46.720 --> 00:40:48.770
and people wanted older people

00:40:48.770 --> 00:40:50.580
to be especially careful and so on.

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:53.030
Well, what else can you deploy?

00:40:53.030 --> 00:40:55.790
So different people have different skills

00:40:55.790 --> 00:40:59.620
that they can deploy or
different resources or whatever.

00:40:59.620 --> 00:41:03.373
So, finding things that bring you joy,

00:41:04.510 --> 00:41:08.360
even if nobody knows
you're the one who did it,

00:41:08.360 --> 00:41:10.640
but they bring you joy.

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:13.447
That I think really important
to our mental health

00:41:13.447 --> 00:41:17.494
and especially important
in times of high stress.

00:41:17.494 --> 00:41:19.040
And it could be cooking a good meal

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:22.350
and, you know, giving
some to the neighbor,

00:41:22.350 --> 00:41:25.360
and it could be making
a flower arrangement

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:30.020
or enjoying the change of
colors or doing a good deed,

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:33.040
saying something positive
to somebody on Twitter.

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:35.041
Any of that, it's all good.

00:41:35.041 --> 00:41:37.120
(all chuckles)

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:38.173
- Does that happen?

00:41:38.173 --> 00:41:40.050
(all chuckles)

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:41.092
- Nobody does that.

00:41:41.092 --> 00:41:43.360
(all chuckles)

00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:45.110
- I was just going to echo that.

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:49.313
And also the part of, I think,

00:41:49.313 --> 00:41:51.960
Huda what you're bringing up.

00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:54.210
And it's also something that we,

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:57.390
I think as a field in mental
health I have had to rethink

00:41:57.390 --> 00:42:01.730
are these ideas that the thought
has to precede the action

00:42:01.730 --> 00:42:03.880
or that the feeling has
to proceed the action,

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:07.440
often is hesitation of, well,
I'm not going to reach out

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:09.500
to somebody until I feel like I'm okay.

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:11.410
Or I'm not going to go
engage in this behavior

00:42:11.410 --> 00:42:14.130
or support someone else or do
something with someone else

00:42:14.130 --> 00:42:16.580
until I feel like I'm doing better.

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:19.800
Or even things that, you
know, brought you joy before,

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:22.100
this hesitation to engage in those

00:42:22.100 --> 00:42:24.430
because like, oh, I'm not feeling it yet.

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:26.480
An increasing realization that actually

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:28.470
it's starting these both behaviors,

00:42:28.470 --> 00:42:29.670
engaging in those behaviors,

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:31.880
especially when they involve other people,

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:34.130
that the positive
emotion at that wellbeing

00:42:34.130 --> 00:42:35.700
comes from the process

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.220
of doing that rather than proceeding it.

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:40.130
And just as I talked about
all that empathy loop

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:41.780
it's really after you've helped somebody

00:42:41.780 --> 00:42:43.660
that that response occurs.

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:46.640
So finding opportunities
just as Huda is saying

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:48.510
with that emotional algebra

00:42:48.510 --> 00:42:50.910
to be able to do things to help others

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:54.137
that give you some type of
reward, rather than saying,

00:42:54.137 --> 00:42:56.220
"Well, I'm going to get
rid of my negatives first

00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:58.500
and then I can go out
and do positive things."

00:42:58.500 --> 00:42:59.787
Throughout the pandemic

00:42:59.787 --> 00:43:01.750
and a recognition of actually
throughout our lives,

00:43:01.750 --> 00:43:03.120
a lot of those negative things

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:05.150
are there, and we are not just the sum

00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:06.210
of those negative things,

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:08.940
we are all of those other
positive things as well.

00:43:08.940 --> 00:43:13.590
And if, you know, my work with
people surviving with PTSD

00:43:13.590 --> 00:43:17.990
from humanitarian conflict,
soldiers has taught me anything,

00:43:17.990 --> 00:43:20.340
in many ways it's not the focus

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:22.680
of take my PTSD symptoms away.

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:23.530
That's not the goal,

00:43:23.530 --> 00:43:26.180
the goal is I want to be
able to help my family,

00:43:26.180 --> 00:43:28.430
I want to be able to interact
with those around me.

00:43:28.430 --> 00:43:31.380
If you can give me those
tools, some of those symptoms,

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:33.440
some of that distress, that's okay,

00:43:33.440 --> 00:43:36.170
but I want to be able to make
a contribution in some way.

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:38.450
So I think encouraging others around us

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:40.100
to make those contributions,

00:43:40.100 --> 00:43:42.180
and it goes back to one of
the points that I made earlier

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:45.940
is part of our role is not
just reaching, you know,

00:43:45.940 --> 00:43:47.650
reaching out to others to help them,

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:50.120
but also asking for help when we need it.

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:52.350
Every time we do that
and tell somebody else

00:43:52.350 --> 00:43:54.300
that we're in distress,
we're worried, we're upset,

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:55.620
we're having a hard time,

00:43:55.620 --> 00:43:57.580
we're giving somebody else an opportunity

00:43:57.580 --> 00:43:59.970
to engage in that empathetic interaction,

00:43:59.970 --> 00:44:01.640
and that they feel better as well.

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:03.200
So that actually asking for help

00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:04.580
is a sign of impr--,

00:44:04.580 --> 00:44:06.780
is an opportunity to improve
things for everybody,

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:08.580
not just for yourself.

00:44:08.580 --> 00:44:10.750
- I think that makes a lot of sense.

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:13.820
I feel like I'm going to keep
an emotional algebra chart

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:15.290
on our (Huda giggles).

00:44:15.290 --> 00:44:16.397
Family wall (laughs).

00:44:20.610 --> 00:44:25.610
- Yeah, no I think I find
using economic economic terms

00:44:26.031 --> 00:44:29.070
for affect regulation to be useful

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:32.540
like do I want to invest
that much negative energy?

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:34.573
Is this worth it?

00:44:34.573 --> 00:44:36.610
Is this, like, you know,
it's an investment.

00:44:36.610 --> 00:44:40.090
All of this is we only have
so many resources within us.

00:44:40.090 --> 00:44:41.860
How do you want to distribute them?

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:43.028
How do you want to use them?

00:44:43.028 --> 00:44:46.861
How do you want to, and that's the part

00:44:46.861 --> 00:44:48.350
that is more personal,

00:44:48.350 --> 00:44:52.140
but can be reinforced,
can be learned together.

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:57.140
And that, you know, that,
I mean, the one thing

00:44:57.200 --> 00:45:00.430
I want to say is building
resilience is a lifelong process,

00:45:00.430 --> 00:45:02.000
you're not born with it,

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:05.430
and you learn from having
developed some how to do more,

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:07.790
but you also learn from watching others,

00:45:07.790 --> 00:45:10.050
they teach you how they survive.

00:45:10.050 --> 00:45:13.700
And so, sharing those
tools are really important.

00:45:13.700 --> 00:45:15.070
And then the other point

00:45:15.070 --> 00:45:17.530
about whether it's top down or bottom up

00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:18.720
is really important.

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:20.650
In Western cultures we're very used

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:23.450
to say thinking that everything comes

00:45:23.450 --> 00:45:25.860
from, to use biological term

00:45:25.860 --> 00:45:27.480
the frontal cortex from thought,

00:45:27.480 --> 00:45:31.460
and then thought must lead
to action and to feelings.

00:45:31.460 --> 00:45:36.250
But if you do any exercise,
any yoga, any meditation,

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:38.780
any breathing, you understand
that there is a lot

00:45:38.780 --> 00:45:41.360
that's biological that bubbles up

00:45:41.360 --> 00:45:43.680
and changes the state of your mind.

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:45.770
And there is also a biology

00:45:45.770 --> 00:45:48.010
to that, a very strong biology

00:45:48.010 --> 00:45:52.370
to that, and that in turn
reinterprets a lot of things.

00:45:52.370 --> 00:45:54.520
So we haven't said very
much about exercise,

00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:56.980
but definitely, definitely,

00:45:56.980 --> 00:45:59.257
and sleeping right, and eating right,

00:45:59.257 --> 00:46:02.210
and you know, everything
that your mother told you,

00:46:02.210 --> 00:46:05.633
but it really does matter,
especially in times of stress.

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:09.470
- It reminds me too of Dr. Kohrt's point

00:46:09.470 --> 00:46:13.270
about the grooming and the
importance of touch, you know,

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:17.620
these sensory experiences
that then can calm our bodies

00:46:17.620 --> 00:46:19.870
and our brains.
- Absolutely.

00:46:19.870 --> 00:46:22.440
- And yeah, it seems like
there are just so many

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:26.773
kind of sensory things
we might think about too.

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:31.338
- That's, also some
people, for some people

00:46:31.338 --> 00:46:34.720
that's really important for that reason.

00:46:34.720 --> 00:46:38.580
So, yeah, I think it, I mean,

00:46:38.580 --> 00:46:43.190
it's been a terrible, terrible
time and so many lives lost

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:44.630
and so many people suffering,

00:46:44.630 --> 00:46:47.500
we will take us a long time to figure out,

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:52.500
and the pandemic of mental
health is just as huge.

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:55.800
But I also think it might
be a teachable moment

00:46:55.800 --> 00:47:00.800
for us as humans, as modern
humans about what matters most.

00:47:01.450 --> 00:47:04.400
You know, and how to bring
together our understanding

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:09.120
from the most biological to
the most social and integrative

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:10.710
and even philosophical.

00:47:10.710 --> 00:47:12.270
If we do it right, we
would learn something

00:47:12.270 --> 00:47:17.090
and we'll come out of it maybe
doing better for each other.

00:47:17.090 --> 00:47:19.980
- You know, earlier you
talked about this idea

00:47:19.980 --> 00:47:23.310
of kind of experiencing stressful events

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:24.690
might buffer you in the future,

00:47:24.690 --> 00:47:26.280
it might teach you how to handle it,

00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:29.053
it might kind of spark a
little more resilience.

00:47:30.070 --> 00:47:33.520
Will we all be better off
in five years in 10 years?

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:34.950
You know, has this experience

00:47:34.950 --> 00:47:37.923
kind of inoculated us in some way?

00:47:39.560 --> 00:47:41.190
Is there a silver lining there?

00:47:41.190 --> 00:47:46.190
- No, this is where I go back
to individual differences

00:47:46.350 --> 00:47:48.720
and how different people
are in different settings

00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:50.240
and different conditions.

00:47:50.240 --> 00:47:53.680
For some people, it will
have toughen them up.

00:47:53.680 --> 00:47:57.707
And when they encounter
something, they say,

00:47:57.707 --> 00:48:00.630
"Hey, you know, I know I've
fended for myself before,

00:48:00.630 --> 00:48:03.880
I can manage it, I know I can survive."

00:48:03.880 --> 00:48:08.020
For others, it might put them
really close to the edge,

00:48:08.020 --> 00:48:10.640
and it won't take very
much for them to fall off,

00:48:10.640 --> 00:48:12.700
or they have fallen off the edge.

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:16.000
And because we all start
in different places,

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:18.230
and we perceive stress differently,

00:48:18.230 --> 00:48:20.890
and we have these different
modules within us,

00:48:20.890 --> 00:48:24.210
some of them biologically programmed,

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:27.410
and some of them through
development and through experience,

00:48:27.410 --> 00:48:30.780
and how this has hit different
people has been different.

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:33.020
So, even though in neuroscience,

00:48:33.020 --> 00:48:36.580
we try to understand the
brain, the circuitry,

00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:39.900
I am somebody who really
cares about differences,

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:42.680
and I think that we need
to be very respectful

00:48:42.680 --> 00:48:46.230
and some people will get
over it and, you know,

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:49.703
thrive for it, and other people
will need a lot of support.

00:48:50.660 --> 00:48:53.109
- And I was just going to echo

00:48:53.109 --> 00:48:55.030
that there is so much diversity.

00:48:55.030 --> 00:48:59.510
And I hope that as we come out
of this and lessons learned

00:48:59.510 --> 00:49:03.630
that we'll also have
more diversity of ways

00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:07.430
that we heal ourselves and
take care of ourselves.

00:49:07.430 --> 00:49:09.640
You know, I think prior to the pandemic,

00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:11.470
being able to do therapy remotely

00:49:11.470 --> 00:49:13.197
was kind of considered taboo

00:49:13.197 --> 00:49:14.690
and you had to be in the same room.

00:49:14.690 --> 00:49:18.123
That hugely limits who has
access to care in some way.

00:49:19.190 --> 00:49:23.270
At the same time I think
another aspect of the pandemic

00:49:23.270 --> 00:49:25.740
has been in how we all
think about mental health.

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:26.960
I think there was already beginning

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:29.520
to be a generational
shift with younger people

00:49:29.520 --> 00:49:31.520
being more open to discuss it,

00:49:31.520 --> 00:49:34.430
but for anybody to come
out of this pandemic

00:49:34.430 --> 00:49:36.447
and not be able to say, you know,

00:49:36.447 --> 00:49:39.430
"I experienced some symptoms
of anxiety or depression

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:41.820
or even trauma in myself,"

00:49:41.820 --> 00:49:44.580
I think would not be very
insightful or reflective.

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:45.910
So hopefully we're also going

00:49:45.910 --> 00:49:48.310
to be continually opening up the doors

00:49:48.310 --> 00:49:50.210
to more honest and frank discussions

00:49:50.210 --> 00:49:52.360
about how we're all doing,
how we're all feeling

00:49:52.360 --> 00:49:54.170
and the supports we need.

00:49:54.170 --> 00:49:56.870
And I think the other area where I hope

00:49:56.870 --> 00:50:00.070
we really come out of this
pandemic in a changed way

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:03.453
is a more diverse,

00:50:03.453 --> 00:50:06.330
comprehensive care system

00:50:06.330 --> 00:50:08.830
that is going to think
about every American

00:50:08.830 --> 00:50:10.850
and every person around the world

00:50:10.850 --> 00:50:13.090
in a more holistic way showing,

00:50:13.090 --> 00:50:14.560
because the pandemic has shown us

00:50:14.560 --> 00:50:17.200
how interconnected we all really are.

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:20.140
And I think, you know, the
other thing is the realization

00:50:20.140 --> 00:50:22.310
that certain things that have been taboo

00:50:22.310 --> 00:50:23.770
in mental health care like touch,

00:50:23.770 --> 00:50:25.170
oh no you would never touch anybody

00:50:25.170 --> 00:50:27.810
that's not related at all,
just as Huda is saying

00:50:27.810 --> 00:50:30.790
and Laura you brought up like,
that's part of our biology.

00:50:30.790 --> 00:50:33.300
So how do we get things like touch

00:50:33.300 --> 00:50:35.380
to be part of our healing process?

00:50:35.380 --> 00:50:37.910
Because we aren't just, you
know, free floating minds,

00:50:37.910 --> 00:50:40.650
we all have bodies and the
bodies are big determinant

00:50:40.650 --> 00:50:42.880
about how we're doing psychologically.

00:50:42.880 --> 00:50:46.280
So I hope these types of
changes and realizations

00:50:46.280 --> 00:50:48.560
will lead to a different way
that we interact with others

00:50:48.560 --> 00:50:50.313
and heal others going forward.

00:50:51.510 --> 00:50:53.770
- And the one thing I
want to add is thinking

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:57.973
more about children and development
in adolescents, I think,

00:50:59.930 --> 00:51:02.650
you know, they're not just
little adults as we know,

00:51:02.650 --> 00:51:07.650
and I have no idea how some
of this is being processed

00:51:07.900 --> 00:51:12.380
by them and how it's
all been impacting them.

00:51:12.380 --> 00:51:15.450
But I hope we have more
resources for mental health

00:51:15.450 --> 00:51:18.000
in children and adolescents,

00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:22.270
and that we learn how to do
better, how to support them,

00:51:22.270 --> 00:51:25.370
and I am really concerned

00:51:26.213 --> 00:51:30.088
about how this is going to play out.

00:51:30.088 --> 00:51:35.088
I think that they deserve
special attention.

00:51:36.380 --> 00:51:38.580
- And I'll just make one final point

00:51:38.580 --> 00:51:41.400
on that and kind of hope
for that next generation.

00:51:41.400 --> 00:51:45.480
As I also think in kids,
that's when empathy, you know,

00:51:45.480 --> 00:51:47.470
begins to be in full bloom,

00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:49.250
and it's really about cultivating

00:51:49.250 --> 00:51:51.700
that and using that in
their lives going forward.

00:51:52.910 --> 00:51:55.910
It's sadly something probably
we spend a lot more time

00:51:55.910 --> 00:51:57.930
restricting our empathy as we get older,

00:51:57.930 --> 00:51:59.620
rather than helping it grow,

00:51:59.620 --> 00:52:02.490
and I think about one
moment during the, you know,

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:04.620
a couple of months
early into the pandemic,

00:52:04.620 --> 00:52:06.153
kids are all at home, I had a, you know,

00:52:06.153 --> 00:52:08.220
I have a four year old daughter,

00:52:08.220 --> 00:52:10.310
my wife and I were arguing as one does

00:52:10.310 --> 00:52:11.600
when you're home with lots of little kids

00:52:11.600 --> 00:52:12.640
in the middle of the pandemic,

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:14.810
and four-year old who
had learned at school

00:52:14.810 --> 00:52:17.660
some deep breathing and
mindfulness exercises

00:52:17.660 --> 00:52:20.130
had this little pinwheel
that she would blow into

00:52:20.130 --> 00:52:22.380
whenever she was upset.

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:23.950
The teachers had taught her to do that.

00:52:23.950 --> 00:52:27.100
And as we were having our
little bit heated discussion,

00:52:27.100 --> 00:52:29.310
she walks over with the
pinwheel and holds it up.

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:32.920
So she could already see
in us, we were distressed,

00:52:32.920 --> 00:52:36.160
we needed some time and she
was there to help heal us.

00:52:36.160 --> 00:52:37.840
So, we got to take care of our kids,

00:52:37.840 --> 00:52:39.830
and we also need to listen and acknowledge

00:52:39.830 --> 00:52:41.800
when they're telling us what's going on,

00:52:41.800 --> 00:52:43.590
because they might be the best messengers

00:52:43.590 --> 00:52:45.630
for us to be a better society,

00:52:45.630 --> 00:52:48.742
be a group that really
cares about one another.

00:52:48.742 --> 00:52:51.690
- I agree, I'm so impressed
with so many of them

00:52:51.690 --> 00:52:55.660
who have been like,
thinking about wearing masks

00:52:55.660 --> 00:52:57.300
because it protects other people,

00:52:57.300 --> 00:53:01.060
or doing things like learning in a way

00:53:01.060 --> 00:53:03.750
that no matter how much
you say it in words

00:53:03.750 --> 00:53:06.130
when it didn't have consequences,

00:53:06.130 --> 00:53:08.630
it kind of, you know goes past,

00:53:08.630 --> 00:53:13.058
but that I think, you know, especially in

00:53:13.058 --> 00:53:14.980
Western culture,

00:53:14.980 --> 00:53:17.210
especially if the kid happens to be lucky

00:53:17.210 --> 00:53:21.410
in a family that is not, you
know, living a distressed life,

00:53:21.410 --> 00:53:23.260
they did not have a realistic view

00:53:23.260 --> 00:53:25.230
of what the world goes through.

00:53:25.230 --> 00:53:27.980
But now I think they have a better idea.

00:53:27.980 --> 00:53:29.150
Because this is how,

00:53:29.150 --> 00:53:31.600
what we're going through
and complaining about.

00:53:31.600 --> 00:53:35.050
I mean, I'm from Syria and
I watched civil war go on

00:53:35.050 --> 00:53:38.350
and those people live
through that and you realize

00:53:38.350 --> 00:53:40.920
the world suffers on a regular basis

00:53:40.920 --> 00:53:44.650
and having an empathy towards
that is not a bad thing

00:53:44.650 --> 00:53:48.200
as long as we can maintain also equanimity

00:53:48.200 --> 00:53:50.763
in mental health through it if possible.

00:53:51.690 --> 00:53:53.630
- That's a really beautiful sentiment.

00:53:53.630 --> 00:53:57.943
I think so much emphasis has
been on like lost academics.

00:53:58.840 --> 00:54:01.330
And I think we forget sometimes

00:54:01.330 --> 00:54:03.760
that there's this whole
emotional piece and empathy piece

00:54:03.760 --> 00:54:06.380
that maybe they've gained something too.

00:54:06.380 --> 00:54:09.280
I don't know, as a parent
myself, that's a nice reminder.

00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:11.390
So I think that is it from us.

00:54:11.390 --> 00:54:12.910
Is there anything either of you want to,

00:54:12.910 --> 00:54:14.060
you know, wrap up with?

00:54:15.090 --> 00:54:17.130
- Well, I wanted to thank you and say,

00:54:17.130 --> 00:54:19.210
I really appreciated the opportunity,

00:54:19.210 --> 00:54:24.150
I liked the fact that it
went from biology to society,

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:27.840
happy 100th anniversary, and, you know,

00:54:27.840 --> 00:54:31.504
I hope this reaches some people
and helps them in some way.

00:54:31.504 --> 00:54:32.990
- And thank you so much
for this opportunity.

00:54:32.990 --> 00:54:35.600
It was great Laura and Sujata
to have this opportunity

00:54:35.600 --> 00:54:38.450
to answer questions and
Huda great hearing from you,

00:54:38.450 --> 00:54:41.740
and I think we have our
next global mission together

00:54:41.740 --> 00:54:46.150
to have the bio-psycho-social
frame of resilience

00:54:46.150 --> 00:54:47.530
for the post pandemic world.

00:54:47.530 --> 00:54:49.920
So thank you so much for this opportunity.

00:54:49.920 --> 00:54:53.180
- Well, if we've set up a
collaboration, great (chuckles).

00:54:53.180 --> 00:54:55.245
This was fascinating,

00:54:55.245 --> 00:54:56.860
I think it was really, really interesting

00:54:56.860 --> 00:54:59.450
to put together the
brain and behavior piece,

00:54:59.450 --> 00:55:01.190
Laura and I don't always
get to work together.

00:55:01.190 --> 00:55:02.590
So that was kind of fun too.

00:55:03.460 --> 00:55:06.050
So thank you so much for joining us.

